  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nCurrent analysis reveals that our area is sandwiched between a 500mb \nridge over the Great Plains and a broad surface high centered over \nthe Ohio River Valley. The pressure gradient at the surface is weak, \nresulting in weak southerly flow while winds at 500mb are out of the \nENE. This veering wind profile along with the relatively dry \nvertical profile will keep clouds off to our west today and support \nafternoon highs in the mid to upper 50s. This pattern will dominate \nthe forecast for most of the week as the 500mb ridge slowly \nprogresses east through the week supporting continued WAA and the \npotential for afternoon highs to reach the upper 60s near 70 by \nFriday. Meanwhile, the surface high will remain mostly stationary \nover the Ohio Valley as a weak surface low and a compact 500mb \ntrough try to move into our area. This will result in a tightening \nof the pressure gradient near the surface and stronger winds (around \n15-20 mph) with the potential for strong gusts (around 20-30 mph) \nespecially for eastern KS and NW MO on Tuesday and Wednesday. Rain \nchances continue to diminish for Thursday as high pressure keeps the \ncompact 500mb trough north of our forecast area with less than a 20% \nchance for rain for far NE KS and far NW MO according to ensemble \nguidance.\n\nBy Friday, we begin to see more of a change in the overall synoptic \npattern. A stronger 500mb trough makes its way east from the Pacific \nNW while the surface high over the Ohio Valley finally shifts east. \nThe 500mb trough will progress east across the Rockies Friday as a \nlow with a trailing cold front develops at the surface. This system \nwill move into the upper Midwest by Saturday morning as pressure \nfalls across our area. The cold front will interact with moist, \nsoutherly flow at the surface, supporting rain chances around 40-60% \nacross the area Saturday into early Sunday morning. There is still \nsome uncertainty on the exact timing of this system with ensemble \nguidance showing the best chance for rain Saturday while \ndeterministic guidance holds of on rain chances until Sunday. The \nchance for severe weather remains low at this time with ensemble \nguidance keeping MUCAPE below 500 J/kg, however, bulk shear values \nare currently around 30-50kts so we will need to continue to monitor \nthe convective environment as this system gets closer. Once the cold \nfront moves through, more seasonable temperatures return early next \nweek with forecasted highs in the mid to upper 40s and lows in the \nupper 20s.",
